<code>assert !\old(this.empty()) => (\result == null && this.peek() == null) || \result.equals(\old(this.peek()));</code>

Explanation:
- The condition `!\old(this.empty())` ensures that the stack was not empty before the `peek` operation.
- The result of `peek()` is stored in `\result`.
- The assertion checks two possibilities:
  1. If the result is `null`, then the last element of the stack (obtained by `this.peek()`) must also be `null`.
  2. Otherwise, the result must be equal to the last element of the stack before the `peek` operation (`\old(this.peek())`). 

This ensures that the behavior of `peek()` is correctly specified according to the comment.
--------------------
<code>assert !\old(this.empty()) => (\result == null && this.peek() == null) || \result.equals(\old(this.peek()));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert !\old(this.empty()) => \result == null && this.peek() == null || \result.equals(\old(this.peek()));</code>

However, since `peek()` throws an exception when the stack is empty, the condition `!\old(this.empty())` ensures that `this.peek()` can be safely called. Therefore, the correct assert statement should be:

<code>assert !\old(this.empty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.peek()));</code>

This ensures that if the stack was not empty before the call, the result of `peek()` is equal to the last element of the stack before the call.
